    <p>Half a century of national championship football in Cape Verde was marked this year with a redesigned trophy, bearing the commemoration of the competition’s fiftieth anniversary. The Federation also confirmed that prize money had been doubled this season, with awards attached to wins, draws, progression through the rounds and the title itself.</p>
<h2>Sal Rei among the champions</h2>
<p>For Sport Sal Rei Club, the anniversary carries a particular meaning. In 2003/2004 the club was crowned National Champion of Cape Verde — the only side from Boa Vista to have taken the title. That season, Sal Rei also produced the championship’s best player, its top scorer and its best coach.</p>
<p>Every year since, the competition has brought together the champions of each sporting region, from Santo Antão in the north to Brava in the south. It remains the stage on which island football measures itself against the whole country.</p>
<h2>A story still being written</h2>
<p>Fifty years of the national championship arrive in the same season that Sal Rei marks seventy-five years of its own. Two anniversaries, one shared history — and, for the club, a reminder that its name already belongs on that list.</p>', 'One island club, one national title — written into fifty years of history.', '[["Anniversary", "50 years"], ["Sal Rei’s title", "2003/2004"], ["Governing body", "Federação Cabo-verdiana de Futebol"]]');
    <p>The Formation School is where the story of many Sal Rei players begins. Built on the idea that a football club carries a responsibility beyond the pitch, the school develops football skills and life skills side by side, across several age groups.</p>
<h2>Football, and more than football</h2>
<p>Training is organised so that the youngest arrivals learn through play and build a love for the game, while older groups work on technical foundations, teamwork and the discipline that carries them towards the senior squad. Throughout, coaches place as much value on respect, commitment and belonging as on results.</p>

<h2>How to enrol</h2>
<p>Families interested in the 2026/27 season can register their children through the club’s social headquarters in Sal Rei. Places are organised by age group, and the coaching staff will guide each new arrival to the right level.</p>
<ul><li>Open to children and young players across several age groups</li><li>Weekly training led by the club’s Formation School coaches</li><li>Enrolment through the club’s social headquarters in Sal Rei</li></ul>
<p>By investing in its academy, Sal Rei invests in Boa Vista. The club encourages families to be part of a project that has helped shape young islanders for decades — and, in this 75th anniversary season, looks ahead to many more.</p>', 'Around 100 young players. One club, shaping the next generation.', '[["Season", "2026/27"], ["Young players", "Around 100"], ["Where to enrol", "Club headquarters"]]');
    <p>On 28 August 1951, a group of young islanders came together in Sal Rei to found what would become Boa Vista’s most decorated football club. Seventy-five years later, the red of Sal Rei is woven into the identity of the town, and the club wants the anniversary to belong to everyone who has worn, watched or supported it.</p>
<h2>A season-long celebration</h2>
<p>The programme opens in August with the Capitão Carlos Nascimento Tournament, the club’s traditional pre-season competition, played this year in honour of one of the great figures of Sal Rei’s history. Across the following months the club will host member gatherings, youth festivals and a series of events that look back at the people and moments that shaped the institution.</p>

<h2>Honouring those who built the club</h2>
<p>A central theme of the anniversary is memory. The club is gathering photographs, trophies and testimonies from former players, coaches and directors, with the aim of preserving the story of Sal Rei for the generations to come. Members are invited to bring their own memories and materials to the club’s social headquarters throughout the season.</p>
<p>The celebrations will also look forward. With the Formation School growing year on year and the first team competing at the top of the regional game, the 75th anniversary is being framed not as an endpoint but as a foundation for the club’s next chapter.</p>
<h2>Key dates</h2>
<ul><li>August — Capitão Carlos Nascimento Tournament</li><li>Throughout the season — member gatherings and youth festivals</li><li>28 August — anniversary of the club’s foundation</li></ul>
<p>Full details of each event will be published on the club’s channels as the season progresses. Sal Rei invites all its members and supporters to be part of a celebration that honours the past while building the future.</p>', 'Seventy-five years of red. One island, one club, one shared history.', '[["Founded", "28 August 1951"], ["Anniversary", "75 years"], ["Where", "Sal Rei, Boa Vista"]]');
    <p>The Taça Bubista is Boa Vista’s cup competition, and for Sal Rei it has been a reliable route back to the national stage: the winner earns the island a place among the country’s best.</p>
<h2>A run that began in 2015</h2>
<p>The first triumph came in 2015/2016, in the same season the club also took the regional championship. Two more followed immediately, in 2016/17 and 2017/18 — three consecutive cups, alongside three consecutive regional titles.</p>
<p>A fourth arrived in 2018/2019, a fifth in 2021/2022, and the sixth in 2024/2025, when the club completed the double with the regional championship and went on to reach the final of the Cape Verde Cup.</p>
<h2>Why it matters</h2>
<p>Across recent seasons Sal Rei has been ever-present in national competitions, qualifying either as Regional Champion or as winner of the Taça Bubista. The cup is not a consolation on Boa Vista — it is one of the two doors to the rest of the country, and Sal Rei has walked through it more often than anyone.</p>', 'Three in a row, then two more — and the sixth in the anniversary season.', '[["Titles", "6"], ["First", "2015/2016"], ["Most recent", "2024/2025"]]');
    <p>A football crest is rarely just a badge. It is what supporters wear, what players carry on the chest, and what a club leaves behind on every pitch it visits. Changing it is never a small decision.</p>
<h2>The board that made the call</h2>
<p>The change was driven by a board made up entirely of women — a leadership that has, season after season, reshaped how the club works and what it expects of itself.</p>
<h2>What followed</h2>
<p>The results arrived quickly. In 2024/2025 Sal Rei were crowned Regional Champions of Boa Vista and won the Taça Bubista, going on to fight all the way to the final of the Cape Verde Cup. The following season the team faced Palmeira in the Cape Verde Super Cup.</p>
<p>Seventy-five years after its foundation, the club carries a crest designed in its own time — and a record that has begun to match the ambition behind it.</p>', 'A new crest, and a board made entirely of women behind it.', '[["Year", "2024"], ["Decision", "Board of directors"], ["Since then", "Regional title & Taça Bubista"]]');
    <p>Cape Verde arrived at their first World Cup as one of the smallest nations in the field and left it with a distinction none of the favourites could claim to have taken from them.</p>
<h2>A goalless night against Spain</h2>
<p>In the group stage, the Blue Sharks held the European champions to a goalless draw in Atlanta. Goalkeeper Vozinha was the decisive figure, keeping the sheet clean with seven saves — the performance that gave Cape Verde the first World Cup point in their history.</p>
<h2>Argentina taken to extra time</h2>
<p>In the Round of 32 in Miami, the reigning champions needed extra time to get past them. Messi opened the scoring, Deroy Duarte levelled just before the hour, and after Lisandro Martínez struck at the start of extra time it was Lopes Cabral who made it 2–2 again on 103 minutes. Only an own goal, with eleven minutes left, decided it.</p>
<h2>What the numbers leave out</h2>
<p>Argentina went on to eliminate Egypt, then Switzerland, then France’s conquerors on the way to the final. Spain came through Belgium and France. Between them the two finalists beat almost everyone they met — but not, within ninety minutes, the islands.</p>
<p>For clubs like ours, that is the part worth keeping. Discipline, organisation and courage travelled from island pitches to the biggest stage in the game, and held their own there.</p>', 'Two finalists. Neither of them beat Cape Verde in ninety minutes.', '[["Spain", "0–0, group stage"], ["Argentina", "2–2 after 90 minutes"], ["Final", "19 July, MetLife Stadium"]]');
    <p>Twelve teams contested this season’s Campeonato Nacional: the champions of each of the eleven sporting regions, plus an additional side, since holders Palmeira entered to defend their title.</p>
<h2>A tie decided from the spot</h2>
<p>África Show, champions of Boa Vista, took a 1–0 lead in the first leg of the semi-final, played in Sal Rei. Palmeira responded with a 2–1 win in the return, levelling the tie at 2–2 on aggregate, and went through 4–2 on penalties.</p>
<p>In the other semi-final, Mindelense — the most decorated club in Cape Verdean football — eliminated Académica do Porto Novo.</p>
<h2>Palmeira retain the title</h2>
<p>The final was staged on 4 July at the Estádio João Serra in Ponta do Sol, on Santo Antão — the first time the ground had hosted the decisive match. After 2–2 through normal time and extra time, Palmeira beat Mindelense 3–2 on penalties, their goalkeeper Djão saving three, to become back-to-back national champions.</p>
<p>It is a familiar name in Sal Rei: it was Palmeira that the club faced in the Cape Verde Super Cup this season. The road to the national stage runs through Boa Vista, and the island has shown once again that it belongs there.</p>', 'Boa Vista football, one penalty shoot-out from a national final.', '[["Competition", "Campeonato Nacional 2025/26"], ["First leg", "Played in Sal Rei"], ["Champions", "Palmeira (Sal)"]]');
    <p>The tournament is a fixture of the Sal Rei calendar, giving the coaching staff a first competitive look at the squad ahead of the regional championship. Played in front of the club’s own supporters at the Arsénio Ramos, it sets the tone for the campaign to come.</p>
<h2>A tribute in every edition</h2>
<p>The competition is named in honour of Capitão Carlos Nascimento, a figure remembered with deep respect across the club. Each edition is an occasion to keep his name and his example alive for younger players and supporters who did not know him.</p>

<h2>What to expect</h2>
<p>The head coach is expected to use the tournament to test combinations across the squad, integrate new additions and build the fitness base for a long season. For supporters, it is the first chance to see the team in action and to welcome back the red of Sal Rei after the summer.</p>
<p>Match dates, opponents and kick-off times will be confirmed on the club’s channels in the coming weeks. Entry details for members will be announced alongside the fixtures.</p>', 'Pre-season starts at home, in front of our own people.', '[["Competition", "Capitão Carlos Nascimento"], ["When", "August 2026"], ["Venue", "Estádio Arsénio Ramos"]]');
    <p>For a country of roughly 525,000 people, spread across an Atlantic archipelago, simply reaching the World Cup was already a landmark. What followed went far beyond that.</p>
<h2>Three draws, and a place in the last 32</h2>
<p>Bubista’s side opened against Spain and held them to a goalless draw. A 2–2 with two-time world champions Uruguay followed, Cape Verde having led before being pegged back and then levelling again after the interval. A third clean-sheet draw, 0–0 against Saudi Arabia, was enough: three points, second place in the group, and qualification behind Spain without a single defeat.</p>
<p>It made Cape Verde the first debutant nation to reach the World Cup knockout phase since Slovakia in 2010, and — by population — the smallest ever to do so.</p>
<h2>Taking Argentina to the limit</h2>
<p>The Round of 32 tie against the defending champions was played in Miami on 3 July. Rather than sit deep, the Blue Sharks pushed forward. Lionel Messi put Argentina ahead before the half hour; captain Ryan Mendes released Deroy Duarte just before the hour and the equaliser arrived. The game went to extra time, where Lisandro Martínez restored Argentina’s lead in the first minute — only for Lopes Cabral to level again on 103 minutes. It took an own goal, eleven minutes from the end of extra time, to separate the sides. It finished 3–2.</p>
<h2>The road that led there</h2>
<p>Qualification had been sealed in October 2025 with a 3–0 win over Eswatini in Praia, in front of a full National Stadium. Cape Verde topped CAF Group D on 23 points, four clear of Cameroon, with goals from Dailon Livramento, Willy Semedo and Stopira sealing the night. By population, only Iceland has ever qualified as a smaller nation.</p>
<p>For every club on every island — Sal Rei included — the run is a reminder of what Cape Verdean football can reach. The players who wore the national shirt this summer began, like ours, on island pitches.</p>', 'The smallest nation ever to reach a World Cup knockout round.', '[["Group H", "Unbeaten in 3"], ["Population", "~525,000"], ["Round of 32", "Argentina 3–2 Cape Verde"]]');
